   "plaque": "aberystwyth_rheidol",
   "title": "Vale of Rheidol Railway",
   "text": "Since 1902 little steam trains have puffed up the Rheidol valley to Devil's Bridge, Pontarfynach. Legend says the Devil built the first bridge there in return for the first soul to cross. A canny old woman threw bread over it, her dog chased it, and the Devil got a dog."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "aberystwyth_red_kites",
   "title": "Red Kites",
   "text": "By the 1930s red kites had vanished from Britain except for a few pairs hiding in the hills of mid Wales. Patient protection brought them back, and now their forked tails are a common sight over Ceredigion. In Welsh it is the barcud coch. Do not wave a sandwich at one."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "aberystwyth_trefechan",
   "title": "Trefechan Bridge",
   "text": "In 1963 young members of Cymdeithas yr Iaith Gymraeg, the Welsh Language Society, sat down on Trefechan Bridge by the harbour to demand official status for Welsh. It was their first big protest. Road signs across Wales are bilingual today, so it seems to have worked."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "aberystwyth_cawl",
   "title": "Cawl",
   "text": "Cawl is the Welsh stew of lamb or beef with leeks, swede, carrots and potatoes, and every family's version is the correct one. Many swear it tastes better the next day as cawl ail-dwym, reheated cawl. Eat it with bread and a good lump of cheese."
  }
